course: dictionary
lesson: 11363
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Abacus is one of these words. Its usual meaning is a frame with rows of beads that slide along wires, used for counting. «Mia slid the beads along the abacus to add 14 and 5.» The beads and the sum tell you which meaning fits.
explain: The second meaning comes from buildings. «The carved abacus sat on top of the tall stone column.» There are no beads here. Architecture is the study of how buildings are designed and built, so this is a building word. Ask yourself: are beads or buildings being talked about?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If «the beads on the abacus show the tens» makes sense, you have the counting meaning. If «the abacus on the column was carved with leaves» makes sense, you have the building meaning.
fact: An abacus is a frame with rows of beads that slide along wires or rods.
fact: People use an abacus for counting and doing sums, and each row can show ones, tens or hundreds.
fact: Abacuses were used in ancient Mesopotamia, Greece, Rome and China.
fact: The Chinese suanpan and the Japanese soroban are counting frames that are still used today.
fact: In architecture, an abacus is the flat slab on top of a column, a building word.
exercise: «Mia slid the beads along the wire to count her sum.» What is an abacus in this sentence? | A flat stone on top of a column | *A frame with rows of beads for counting | A type of ancient coin | A necklace made of beads
exercise: «The carved abacus sat on top of the tall column.» In this sentence, what does abacus mean? | A kind of stone wall | *The flat slab on top of a column | A frame with beads on wires | A rod for sweeping floors
exercise: Which word is the name of the Chinese counting frame? | *suanpan | abacus-frame | soroban | column
test: «Leo counted the tens on the abacus and got 40.» What do the rows of beads on an abacus show? | Names of towns | Days of the week | Colours of the rainbow | *Ones, tens and hundreds
test: «The carved abacus on the column was covered in leaves.» What does abacus mean here? | A bead frame for counting | A kind of carpet | *The flat top of a column | A school bag
exam: Ana reads «He moved the beads on the abacus» and «the abacus was carved with leaves». What does abacus mean in each? | *A bead frame in the first, the top of a column in the second | A column in the first, a bead frame in the second | Both mean a kind of stone wall | Both mean a bead frame
